  "live_records": {
    "q-95845": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95845",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\int (5+2{x}^{2}-5{tanh}^{2}⁡(4x-1))\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1-{tanh}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sech}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nSetting $\\theta =4x-1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{tanh}^{2}⁡(4x-1) & ={sech}^{2}⁡(4x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5+2{x}^{2}-5{tanh}^{2}⁡(4x-1))\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2{x}^{2}+5-5{tanh}^{2}⁡(4x-1))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2{x}^{2}+5[1-{tanh}^{2}⁡(4x-1)])\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2{x}^{2}+5{sech}^{2}⁡(4x-1))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+5(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(4x-1))+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(4x-1)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
