      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}({coth}^{2}⁡x-{coth}^{2}⁡x{tanh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the secant-tangent identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic secant-tangent identity\n\n$$\n1-{tanh}^{2}⁡x={sech}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the integral and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}({coth}^{2}⁡x-{coth}^{2}⁡x{tanh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{coth}^{2}⁡x\\cdot (1-{tanh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{coth}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {sech}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x{|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)-\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}-\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+{2}^{-1}}{2-{2}^{-1}}-\\frac{4+{4}^{-1}}{4-{4}^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}-\\frac{17}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
